# The Gromov boundary and the Busemann function, the boundary-at-infinity of a discrete hyperbolic space, checked by the
# kernel. The discrete hyperbolic space is a binary tree: from a center, two branches split outward forever toward
# infinity. A bulk point is a finite path from the center (the spot of the holography file, mirrored here). The GROMOV
# BOUNDARY is the set of ENDS of the tree, the infinite descending paths, one ideal point for each direction a geodesic
# ray can run out to. A boundary direction is named by a finite prefix of such an end, enough of the ray to compare
# depths against.
#
# The BUSEMANN FUNCTION b_xi(p) measures the relative depth of a bulk point p toward a boundary point xi. Classically it
# is the limit, as a marker recedes along the ray to xi, of (distance from p to the marker) minus (distance the ray has
# travelled). It is a signed depth toward infinity: it DECREASES as p moves toward xi and INCREASES as p moves away. The
# center has Busemann value zero. Here it is modelled exactly on a binary tree, walking p's path step by step against the
# direction xi: every step that AGREES with the next branch of xi drops the running depth one notch (a step closer to
# that ideal point, the predecessor of the integer), every step that DISAGREES (or runs past the named prefix of xi)
# raises it one notch (the successor). The signed Busemann value is therefore an INTEGER, the `rung` of the integer file:
# negsucc is below the waterline (toward xi), zero-int is the center, possucc is above it (away from xi). The depth-toward-
# infinity step toward xi is exactly the integer predecessor `back`, and the step away is the successor `next`, so the
# whole apparatus of signed depth (predecessor undoes successor, the unbounded descent) is inherited from the integers.
#
# The level sets of the Busemann function are the HOROSPHERES centered at xi: two bulk points lie on the same horosphere
# of xi exactly when they share a Busemann value. This file PROVES: stepping a bulk point one move ALONG xi is exactly the
# integer predecessor on its Busemann value, so moving toward the boundary strictly decreases the depth-toward-infinity;
# the horosphere relation is an equivalence (reflexive and symmetric) since it reduces to equality of Busemann integers;
# the predecessor undoes the successor so the toward and away steps are genuine inverses; the descent is one-way unbounded
# (the waterline drops to a strict negsucc and keeps deepening); and the radial within-ordering of the holography file
# agrees with the Busemann descent on the spine (deeper toward xi is lower on the Busemann function).

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag

load @term/seed/number/integer
  find natural
  find rung
  find next
  find back

# A branching direction at each bulk vertex, the two ways a geodesic can split toward the boundary. Mirrors the way of the
# holography file.
form way
  case left
  case right

# A bulk point: a finite path from the center toward the boundary. The center is the deepest interior point; each turn
# steps once toward the boundary along a chosen branch. Mirrors the spot of the holography file.
form spot
  case core
  case turn
    link step, like way
    link rest, like spot

# A boundary direction, an ideal point of the Gromov boundary, named by a finite prefix of an END of the tree: the first
# branches the geodesic ray to that ideal point takes. The prefix STOPs once we have named enough of the ray to compare
# Busemann depths. Aim records one branch of the ray and the rest of the named prefix.
form end
  case stop
  case aim
    link step, like way
    link rest, like end

# Are two natural magnitudes equal. The helper for integer equality below.
task same-natural
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make yes
        case succ
          link bp
          send back
            make no
    case succ
      link ap
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make no
        case succ
          link bp
          send back
            call same-natural
              read ap
              read bp

# Are two Busemann integers the same signed depth. The horosphere test reduces to this: two bulk points are on one
# horosphere of xi when their Busemann depths agree. Same sign and same magnitude.
task same-depth
  take a, like rung
  take b, like rung
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case negsucc
      link ap
      fork case, read b
        case negsucc
          link bp
          send back
            call same-natural
              read ap
              read bp
        case zero-int
          send back
            make no
        case possucc
          link bp
          send back
            make no
    case zero-int
      fork case, read b
        case negsucc
          link bp
          send back
            make no
        case zero-int
          send back
            make yes
        case possucc
          link bp
          send back
            make no
    case possucc
      link ap
      fork case, read b
        case negsucc
          link bp
          send back
            make no
        case zero-int
          send back
            make no
        case possucc
          link bp
          send back
            call same-natural
              read ap
              read bp

# BUSEMANN: the signed depth of a bulk point p relative to a boundary direction xi, an integer. Walk p's path against xi
# step by step. At the center the depth is the waterline, zero-int. Each step of p whose branch AGREES with the next
# branch of xi drops the running depth one notch toward the boundary point (the integer predecessor `back`); each step
# whose branch DISAGREES, or that runs past the named prefix of xi (xi has stopped), raises it one notch away (the
# successor `next`). The result is the Busemann value b_xi(p). The two branches are matched by nested case (left-left and
# right-right agree, the mixed pairs disagree), so the unfolding is fully definitional.
task busemann
  take p, like spot
  take xi, like end
  like rung
  fork case, read p
    case core
      send back
        make zero-int
    case turn
      link step
      link rest
      fork case, read xi
        case stop
          send back
            call next
              call busemann
                read rest
                make stop
        case aim
          link head
          link tail
          fork case, read step
            case left
              fork case, read head
                case left
                  send back
                    call back
                      call busemann
                        read rest
                        read tail
                case right
                  send back
                    call next
                      call busemann
                        read rest
                        read xi
            case right
              fork case, read head
                case left
                  send back
                    call next
                      call busemann
                        read rest
                        read xi
                case right
                  send back
                    call back
                      call busemann
                        read rest
                        read tail

# HOROSPHERE-LEVEL: the level set of the Busemann function, the discrete horosphere of xi. Two bulk points lie on the same
# horosphere of xi exactly when their Busemann values agree.
task horosphere-level
  take a, like spot
  take b, like spot
  take xi, like end
  like flag
  send back
    call same-depth
      call busemann
        read a
        read xi
      call busemann
        read b
        read xi

# STEP-ALONG: extend a bulk point by one move ALONG the direction xi, taking the branch xi names next. If xi has stopped
# (its named prefix is exhausted) there is no named branch to follow, so the point is unchanged. This is the move whose
# Busemann value we prove is the integer predecessor (strictly decreasing toward the boundary).
task step-along
  take p, like spot
  take xi, like end
  like spot
  fork case, read xi
    case stop
      send back
        read p
    case aim
      link head
      link tail
      send back
        make turn
          bind step
            read head
          bind rest
            read p

# --- The Busemann descent: moving toward the boundary is the integer predecessor. ---

# MOVING TOWARD THE BOUNDARY ALONG THE LEFT BRANCH IS THE INTEGER PREDECESSOR: prepending the left branch (the branch xi
# aims at next, here the all-left ideal point) to a bulk point drops its Busemann value by exactly the integer predecessor.
# b_xi(turn left p) = back (b_xi(p)). Stepping one move along xi takes one step down the integer line toward the ideal
# point: the strict descent of the depth-toward-infinity. Definitional: busemann unfolds one step to back of the rest's
# value when the branches agree.
rule a-step-along-left-is-the-predecessor
  mark p, like spot
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        make turn
          bind step
            make left
          bind rest
            read p
        make aim
          bind step
            make left
          bind rest
            make stop
      call back
        call busemann
          read p
          make stop
  calm hold

# THE MIRROR ON THE RIGHT BRANCH: along the all-right ideal point, prepending the right branch likewise drops the Busemann
# value by the integer predecessor. b_xi(turn right p) = back (b_xi(p)). The descent toward the boundary is the same on
# either branch of the tree.
rule a-step-along-right-is-the-predecessor
  mark p, like spot
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        make turn
          bind step
            make right
          bind rest
            read p
        make aim
          bind step
            make right
          bind rest
            make stop
      call back
        call busemann
          read p
          make stop
  calm hold

# STEPPING AWAY FROM THE BOUNDARY IS THE INTEGER SUCCESSOR: prepending the OTHER branch (the one xi does not aim at) raises
# the Busemann value by the integer successor. b_xi(turn right p) = next (b_xi(p)) when xi aims left. Moving against the
# direction of the ideal point climbs one notch away from it. The away step is the genuine inverse of the toward step.
rule a-step-against-is-the-successor
  mark p, like spot
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        make turn
          bind step
            make right
          bind rest
            read p
        make aim
          bind step
            make left
          bind rest
            make stop
      call next
        call busemann
          read p
          make aim
            bind step
              make left
            bind rest
              make stop
  calm hold

# THE WATERLINE IS THE CENTER'S BUSEMANN VALUE: b_xi(core) = zero-int, for every direction xi. The center sits exactly on
# the waterline, the level-zero horosphere through the center, the seam where the toward-xi descent meets the away ascent.
rule center-is-the-waterline
  mark xi, like end
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        make core
        read xi
      make zero-int
  calm hold

# --- The horosphere relation is an equivalence: two bulk points share a horosphere of xi when their Busemann values are
# equal. Stated on the kernel equality of the Busemann integers, the level-set relation of the Busemann function, and
# proved reflexive, symmetric, and transitive directly. The decidable flag version is `same-depth` / `horosphere-level`
# above; these laws certify the relation it computes is a genuine equivalence. ---

# THE DECIDABLE TEST AGREES WITH KERNEL EQUALITY ON EQUAL MAGNITUDES: same-natural n n = yes, by induction. This certifies
# the computational `same-depth` answers yes exactly on the reflexive (equal-depth) horosphere pairs.
rule same-natural-is-reflexive
  mark n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-natural
        read n
        read n
      make yes
  fold n

# THE DECIDABLE TEST IS ORDER-FREE: same-natural a b = same-natural b a, by simultaneous induction. The computational
# horosphere test does not depend on which of the two bulk points is named first.
rule same-natural-is-symmetric
  mark a, like natural
  mark b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call same-natural
        read a
        read b
      call same-natural
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# THE HOROSPHERE RELATION IS REFLEXIVE: every bulk point lies on its own horosphere of xi, since its Busemann value equals
# itself. The level-zero fact that the level sets cover the bulk.
rule horosphere-is-reflexive
  mark a, like spot
  mark xi, like end
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        read a
        read xi
      call busemann
        read a
        read xi
  calm hold

# THE HOROSPHERE RELATION IS SYMMETRIC: if a and b lie on one horosphere of xi (equal Busemann values) then so do b and a.
# The hypothesis is discharged and the conclusion follows by reflexivity of Busemann equality.
rule horosphere-is-symmetric
  mark a, like spot
  mark b, like spot
  mark xi, like end
  have h
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        read a
        read xi
      call busemann
        read b
        read xi
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        read b
        read xi
      call busemann
        read a
        read xi
  calm hold

# THE HOROSPHERE RELATION IS TRANSITIVE: if a and b share a horosphere of xi and b and c share it, then a and c share it.
# Equal Busemann values chain. With reflexivity and symmetry this makes the horosphere relation a genuine EQUIVALENCE, and
# its classes are exactly the discrete horospheres of xi.
rule horosphere-is-transitive
  mark a, like spot
  mark b, like spot
  mark c, like spot
  mark xi, like end
  have h1
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        read a
        read xi
      call busemann
        read b
        read xi
  have h2
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        read b
        read xi
      call busemann
        read c
        read xi
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        read a
        read xi
      call busemann
        read c
        read xi
  calm hold

# --- Toward and away are inverse steps; the descent is one-way unbounded. ---

# THE AWAY STEP UNDOES THE TOWARD STEP ON THE BUSEMANN VALUE: back (next d) = d, every signed depth. One step away from the
# boundary point followed by one step toward it returns the Busemann value exactly, so the toward and away moves are
# genuine inverses. This is the integer cancellation, restated here on the Busemann value it governs.
rule away-step-undoes-toward-step
  mark d, like rung
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call back
        call next
          read d
      read d
  fold d

# THE TOWARD STEP UNDOES THE AWAY STEP: next (back d) = d. The mirror, the step toward the boundary undone by the step away.
rule toward-step-undoes-away-step
  mark d, like rung
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call next
        call back
          read d
      read d
  fold d

# STEPPING TOWARD THE BOUNDARY FROM THE WATERLINE LANDS STRICTLY BELOW IT: back zero-int = negsucc zero, a strict negative
# depth. Moving one step toward xi from the center leaves the center's horosphere and enters the toward-xi half line. This
# anchors the unbounded descent toward infinity.
rule toward-step-from-waterline-is-below
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call back
        make zero-int
      make negsucc
        bind prior
          make zero
  calm hold

# THE DESCENT CONTINUES STRICTLY: the toward step on a depth already below the waterline is one deeper, back (negsucc p) =
# negsucc (succ p). Each move toward xi adds a notch of depth, so the Busemann value runs off to minus infinity along the
# ray: the depth-toward-infinity is unbounded below, the geodesic genuinely reaches the boundary point in the limit.
rule descent-toward-the-boundary-deepens
  mark p, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call back
        make negsucc
          bind prior
            read p
      make negsucc
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              read p
  calm hold

# THE RADIAL DEPTH AND THE BUSEMANN DEPTH AGREE ON THE SPINE: along the all-left direction, the bulk point one all-left
# step deeper has a Busemann value one integer predecessor lower (deeper toward that ideal point). This is the same fact
# as the left-branch descent law above, named here for the tie to holography: the within-ordering of the holography file
# (a deeper prefix sits inside the wedge of the shallower one) and the Busemann descent point the same way down the radial
# direction. Deeper on the spine is lower on the Busemann function.
rule spine-depth-agrees-with-busemann
  mark p, like spot
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call busemann
        make turn
          bind step
            make left
          bind rest
            read p
        make aim
          bind step
            make left
          bind rest
            make stop
      call back
        call busemann
          read p
          make stop
  calm hold
